{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"f57d8b7e-fd5a-4daf-9378-2e3d21342f7c","hash":"dee84daf3ef860eca952a22b3d4f4abc17278a135d2a7aa3425dbe38d6a3e1f0","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LvNftjvwpvgPFDtPVQnmmT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/pricing-implementation-plan-2026-11.md","offset":14,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BRwveFuNzZ97Zof8JvdZFd","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"uptime; pgrep -fl ninja | head; A=/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/credits-onboarding; cd $A; export PATH=/Applications/CMake.app/Contents/bin:/Users/kenneth/.nvm/versions/node/v24.13.0/bin:$PATH; cd src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/AgentUI; npx tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | tail -5 | cut -c1-160; npx vitest run 2>&1 | tail -5 | cut -c1-160; grep -n \"composer-credits\\|credit-panel-total\\|attachment-sub\\b\" src/styles.css | head -5; sed -n 1926,1938p src/styles.css","description":"Check machine load and re-run Agent page tests","timeout":120000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"f57d8b7e-fd5a-4daf-9378-2e3d21342f7c","hash":"46dfa21bcf1be0b3690a0a0fc6e51ac9990a2bbe56b8a52a7665f8c39242619a","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LvNftjvwpvgPFDtPVQnmmT","type":"tool_result","content":"14\tEvery \"recommend\" below is my opinion; every \"decided\" is Kenneth's. Code facts were read from the checkouts on 2026-11-01 (tsd-enterprise master, JusPrin `jusprin-newui` at 69780c7af4, ops master).\n15\t\n16\t## 1. The plan being implemented\n17\t\n18\t- The unit is a **credit**, about 2 cents (decided 2026-11-01: \"credits\", not the placeholder \"coins\"). Credits pay per message, not per token. A **Standard** message (DeepSeek V4 Flash class) costs 1 credit; an **Expert** message (Claude Sonnet 5.5 class, not Opus) costs 5 credits. The 1:5 ratio is decided. A balance is shown in credits only, \"562 credits left\", never with a count of messages beside it (Kenneth, 2026-11-02: the two numbers together are confusing). Prices in messages appear only on the picker options, the stop card, the pricing page sentence and one scale line under the first-month offer.\n19\t- **Pay as you go** (the pack): 500 credits for $10, never expire. Optional auto-reload at a discount.\n20\t- **Hobby, $10 a month:** 750 credits, Standard messages cost credits, unused credits lost at the end of each month.\n21\t- **Workshop, $20 a month:** 1,000 credits plus unlimited Standard messages, unused credits lost each month. Needs fair-use wording.\n22\t- Plan names decided 2026-11-01: Hobby, Workshop, Pay as you go. No Pro, Plus or Premium, because Obico Pro exists next door.\n23\t- **Obico subscribers:** 20% off both subscriptions.\n24\t- **Your first month** (the sign-up grant, never called a trial): about $10 of credits (500), expiring after one month. **Trickle:** a free monthly Standard allowance for light users.\n25\t- **Rewards:** credits of real value for a print report with a photo and detailed text, with a human review flag for bad ones.\n26\t- **Launch period:** discounted, not free, credits.\n27\t- **Alert:** tell the person when their usage has grown to where a subscription saves them money.\n28\t- **BYOK** (the person's own API key) stays free with no sign-in.\n29\t\n30\t### The cost figures the margins rest on\n31\t\n32\tMeasured per user message, one run per journey, from `journey-cost-2026-10.md` (Sonnet added 2026-11-01, so the peer session's \"Sonnet still unmeasured\" is out of date):\n33\t\n34\t| Tier | Model | Cost per user message | Price at 2¢ a credit | Gross margin |\n35\t|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n36\t| Standard | DeepSeek V4 Flash (OpenRouter) | 0.4¢ to 1¢; 5¢ when the agent polled the slicer 75 times | 2¢ (1 credit) | 50% to 80%; negative in the polling case |\n37\t| Expert | Claude Sonnet 5.5 | 5.6¢ to 7.4¢, mean 6.3¢ over 14 messages | 10¢ (5 credits) | 26% to 44%, 37% at the mean |\n38\t| (not offered) | Claude Opus 5.5 | 10¢ to 21¢ | 10¢ | negative |\n39\t\n40\t\"Per user message\" divides a whole session's cost, including the turns the app starts by itself (file opened, slice finished), by the messages the person typed. So the margins above already assume app-started turns are not charged (see section 3.3). Two cost prerequisites move these: Anthropic's 5-minute cache expiry during a slow slice rewrote 72k tokens for $0.36 on Opus (section 7.1), and the slicer polling tripled one basic session (section 7.2).\n41\t\n42\t## 2. What exists today\n43\t\n44\t**Gateway.** `web_ent/jusprin/v2/gateway.py` is a raw ASGI endpoint on daphne (`tsdws-prod`) that streams `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/messages` (Anthropic format) and `/responses` (OpenAI format) to an upstream model. `credits.py` admits each turn: a Redis rate limit of 60 turns a minute per person (`JUSPRIN_V2_TURNS_PER_MINUTE`), then one v1 credit per turn through `consume_credit_for_pipeline` in `obico-server/backend/app/jusprin/ai_credits.py`, which decrements `JusPrinAICredit.ai_credit_used_current_month` against `ai_credit_free_monthly_quota` (50 a month, -1 for unlimited). A turn is every request that carries the same `X-JusPrin-Turn` header; title requests (`X-JusPrin-Request: title`) are free. The Redis keys `seen` and `charged` make the charge idempotent per turn for an hour. Exhaustion is a 402 with code `quota_exhausted`.\n45\t\n46\t**Aliases.** `aliases.py` reads `JUSPRIN_MODEL_ALIASES` (a ConfigMap, `ops/tsd/prod/jusprin-v2-gateway.yaml`). Production has one alias, `slicer-1`, label \"Slicer 1\", efforts fast/thorough, Anthropic backend `claude-sonnet-5-5` at 100%, OpenAI backend `gpt-6.1-sol` at 0%. Only the formats `anthropic` and `responses` exist. There is no DeepSeek backend and no image stripping.\n47\t\n48\t**Account.** `GET /jusprin/api/me/` (obico-server `JusPrinMeViewSet`) answers `{\"user\": {...}, \"ai_credits\": {...}}`; the desktop app reads only the email (`CloudAccount.cpp:233`). `GET /ent/jusprin/v2/models` lists aliases with `format`, `efforts`, `default_effort`.\n49\t\n50\t**Billing.** Chargebee, site `obico`. v1 sells `jusprin-unlimited-monthly` ($6.99) and `jusprin-unlimited-annual` ($59.99) through `web_ent/jusprin/jusprin_cb_views.py`: hosted checkout pages, a callback, a webhook that upserts `JusPrinPlan` and flips the v1 quota to -1. An Obico Pro discount coupon (30%) is applied at checkout after a live Chargebee status call. Obico's own code (`web_ent/app_ent/cb_views.py`, `detective_hour_accounting.py`) already has the two patterns the credit ledger needs: one-time packs sold as a non-recurring add-on on a placeholder subscription (`pre-paid-detective-hour-pack`, credited from the `invoice_generated` webhook, keyed `inv:<invoice id>`), and per-term included credits keyed `sub:<resource_version>:<suffix>` so a repeated webhook never double-credits. `DetectiveHourTransaction` is a ledger of signed amounts with a `forfeitable` flag; balance is a sum.\n51\t\n52\t**Obico subscriber check.** `app_ent.models.Subscription.active()` on `user.subscription`, mirrored to `user.is_pro`. Local, no Chargebee call needed.\n53\t\n54\t**Rewards precedent.** `credit_dh_for_contribution` (a Celery task) grants non-forfeitable detection hours for labelling print frames; `PrintShotFeedbackReview.invalid` is the per-label human review flag set in the admin.\n55\t\n56\t**Desktop app.** `ProviderProfile.hpp` row `jusprin` (default model `slicer-1`, `account_sign_in` true) makes `ProviderAgent` send `X-JusPrin-Turn`, `X-JusPrin-Request` (reply or title) and `X-JusPrin-Chat`. `ProviderAgent::http_error` maps 402 to `quota_exhausted` with the text \"Your JusPrin account has used its Agent allowance for now. See your account for more.\" The Agent page gets `model_catalog` (per provider: models with `efforts`), `chat_model`, `account_status` (sign-in phase only) and `setup_keys` (the signed-in email). `ModelPickers.tsx` draws the per-chat model and effort pickers; `Preferences.tsx` has an `account` section. Print records (`ManufacturingHistory.hpp`, `GcodeRecord` with destinations and prints) live in the project file; nothing about them reaches the server.\n57\t\n58\t**Cache control.** `AnthropicCodec.cpp` sets `cache_control: {type: ephemeral}` with no `ttl` on the last tool, the system text and the last user message. The append-only chat record (commit 1635438d4f) already makes the slice-finished turn read the whole prior prompt from cache, so only expiry during a pause remains.\n59\t\n60\t**Slice polling.** `slice_start`'s result note already tells the model \"Checking on it from this turn shows nothing the person can't already see\" (`ToolExecutionCoordinator.cpp:2155`). DeepSeek polled `workspace_inspect` anyway, 75 times in one run and twice in another.\n61\t\n62\t## 3. Gateway: the credit ledger\n63\t\n64\tNew module `web_ent/jusprin/ledger/` (models, ledger, webhooks, tests), replacing the `consume_credit_for_pipeline` call in `credits.py` for v2 only. v1 keeps `JusPrinAICredit` untouched; the v1 app never reaches these paths.\n65\t\n66\t### 3.1 Tables\n67\t\n68\t```\n69\tCreditGrant            one lot of credits\n70\t  user, kind {pack, subscription, signup, trickle, reward, launch_bonus, adjustment}\n71\t  credits_granted, credits_remaining\n72\t  expires_at          null = never (packs, rewards)\n73\t  basic_only          bool (trickle lots pay basic turns only)\n74\t  source_ref          unique per user: inv:<invoice>, sub:<resource_version>:credits,\n75\t                      signup, trickle:<yyyy-mm>, report:<print report id>\n76\t  created_at\n77\t\n78\tCreditCharge           one per charged turn\n79\t  user, turn_id      unique together\n80\t  alias, tier {basic, expert}, credits (0 for a turn covered by unlimited basic or a free app turn)\n81\t  origin {person, app}\n82\t  created_at         indexed with user\n83\t\n84\tCreditAllocation       which lots paid a charge (for refunds and the balance breakdown)\n85\t  charge, grant, credits\n86\t```\n87\t\n88\t`JusPrinPlan` (already there, one row per user) becomes the v2 subscription record too: add `current_term_start`, `current_term_end`, `external_resource_version` as Obico's `Subscription` has, and treat `plan_id` prefixes `jusprin-credits-` as v2 plans. v1's `jusprin-unlimited-` plans stay recognised.\n89\t\n90\t### 3.2 Charging a turn\n91\t\n92\t`ledger.charge_turn(user_id, turn_id, alias, origin)`, called from `credits.admit` in place of the v1 call, inside `transaction.atomic()` with `select_for_update()` on the person's live lots:\n93\t\n94\t1. Cost = the alias's `credits` (section 3.4). Origin `app` costs 0 (section 3.3). Tier basic on an active Workshop costs 0 until the fair-use ceiling (section 6), then 1.\n95\t2. Lazy trickle: if no `trickle:<this month>` lot exists for the person, create it first (section 3.5). This replaces a monthly sweep over 250k accounts.\n96\t3. Draw order: lots that expire soonest first, never-expiring lots last. Among lots with the same expiry, oldest first. `basic_only` lots are skipped for expert turns. So a sign-up lot (30 days) is spent before a subscription lot (term end), and both before a pack.\n97\t4. Not enough credits: raise so `admit` returns a new `Admission.CREDITS_EXHAUSTED`, which the gateway answers as 402 with code `credits_exhausted` and a body the client can act on: `{\"balance\": 3, \"cost\": 5, \"basic_only_credits\": 0, \"affordable_aliases\": [\"slicer-basic\"], \"plan\": null}`. `basic_only_credits` is what the trickle lot still holds; the card offers \"New Standard chat\" only when it is 5 or more (board 3d), so `affordable_aliases` alone is not the test. `errors.py` grows that entry; the old `quota_exhausted` text stays for v1.\n98\t5. Record `CreditCharge` and its allocations. The Redis `charged` key stays as the fast path; the unique `(user, turn_id)` is the truth, so a retried first request of a turn never pays twice.\n99\t\n100\tOnly the first request of a turn charges, as today, so tool-call follow-ups inside a turn are free however many there are. This is what makes \"per message\" honest and also why the polling fix matters: follow-ups cost the gateway real money while charging nothing.\n101\t\n102\t### 3.3 Which turns are charged (recommend)\n103\t\n104\tCharge only turns the person started. The app also starts turns by itself: the file-opened report, the slice-finished note, print-issue and setup-changed skills. Those are the agent doing its job unasked, and the measured per-message costs already include them, so they fit the margin table at 0 credits. The client tells the gateway with a third value of the existing header, `X-JusPrin-Request: note`, set by `ProviderAgent` from a new `turn_origin` on the transcript (the host knows which message id started the turn: `start_turn(note.id)` in `AgentHost.cpp:1836` versus a person's message).\n105\t\n106\tGuard: the Redis minute limit already caps 60 turns a minute. Add a daily counter of free app turns per person (Redis, 24 h) and charge them as basic past a daily ceiling. 50 a day is a placeholder: the four harness sessions started 2 to 4 app turns each, and no engaged-usage source exists yet for how many a heavy day starts. Set it from the first month of gateway charge records.\n107\t\n108\t**Which model an app-started turn uses (decided by Kenneth, 2026-11-02):** always the account's Expert alias, whatever the chat's model is or will be, because the file-opened report has to read the plate picture. It costs 0 credits as above, and it does not fix the chat's model: today `use_chat_model` stores the chat's first choice on any turn, including the app's note, so a chat that begins with a file is pinned to the default before the person can choose. It must store the choice only on a turn the person started, and the app turn sends the Expert alias directly. A chat may therefore hold one Expert turn before Standard ones; `prepare()` strips the pictures from that history for the Standard backend (3.4).\n109\t\n110\t### 3.4 Tiers in the alias config\n111\t\n112\t`aliases.py` gains two required keys per alias, `tier` (\"basic\" or \"expert\"; the config value stays `basic`, the person sees the label \"Standard\") and `credits` (integer), and one optional backend key, `images` (default true). `GET /models` reports `tier` and `credits` so the client can print \"5 credits a message\" in the picker menu and the tooltip without hard-coding prices anywhere. The price is per alias, not per effort: Fast and Thorough cost the same within a tier, as the boards draw it (Expert defaults to Thorough, Standard to Fast).\n113\t\n114\tProduction aliases (recommend):\n115\t\n116\t| id | label | tier | credits | backend |\n117\t|---|---|---|---:|---|\n118\t| `slicer-1` | Expert | expert | 5 | Anthropic `claude-sonnet-5-5` (unchanged, so every existing chat pinned to it keeps working) |\n119\t| `slicer-basic` | Standard | basic | 1 | OpenRouter `deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash` in the `responses` format, `images: false` |\n120\t\n121\tKeeping the id `slicer-1` avoids an `unknown_model` for chats that already exist; only its label changes. The client's default model is the `ProviderProfile` row's `slicer-1`, so new chats default to Expert. Whether a new chat should default to Expert or Standard is a product decision; I recommend Expert while the person has credits, because the sign-up grant is meant to show the best version, and the picker makes Standard one click away.\n122\t\n123\tDeepSeek work the gateway needs: `upstream.py`'s `prepare()` strips image blocks from a request when the chosen backend has `images: false` (the measurement proxy did this; the model's hosts reject image input), and leaves a text note in their place so the model knows a picture was dropped. OpenRouter speaks the Responses API, so no new wire format is needed. First-party DeepSeek is cheaper per cached token but speaks chat-completions and serves a different model under the same name; not for launch.\n124\t\n125\t### 3.5 Lots that are not bought\n126\t\n127\t- **Your first month** (decided: about $10, one month, not called a trial): 500 credits, `expires_at = now + 30 days`, `source_ref = signup`, created on the person's first v2 request or first `/models` call when they have no lot at all. One per account, ever. No device checks; Obico sign-up already requires a verified email, and a second account to farm another $5 of model cost is not worth defending against now.\n128\t- **Trickle** (decided in principle; size to choose): a monthly lot of `basic_only` credits expiring at the end of the calendar month, created lazily (3.2 step 2). The size, 50, is a placeholder: v1's free-user figures (median 2 chats a month, 90th percentile 6) are a floor from a product people had stopped using, not an estimate of a light v2 user, so there is no engaged-usage source for this number yet. At 50 the cost is at most about 50¢ a month per person; set the real size from the first month of charge records.\n129\t- **Rewards**: section 8.\n130\t- **Launch bonus** (decided: discounted, never free): recommend doubling the credits of every pack bought before a date in a setting, `JUSPRIN_LAUNCH_DOUBLE_UNTIL`, as a second lot `kind = launch_bonus` on the same invoice ref. It needs no Chargebee change and works the same for any payment method added later. A Chargebee coupon on the pack is the alternative if Kenneth prefers the discount to show on the receipt.\n131\t\n132\t### 3.6 Expiry\n133\t\n134\tExpiry is a comparison at read and charge time (`expires_at > now`), never a sweep, so a lot simply stops counting. A nightly task zeroes `credits_remaining` on expired lots and writes the forfeited count into Langfuse or a metric, which is the \"how much of their quota do subscribers use\" number Kenneth expects to be under half.\n135\t\n136\t## 4. Billing: Chargebee\n137\t\n138\tReuse the two patterns Obico already runs.\n139\t\n140\t- **Packs:** plan `jusprin-credit-pack` ($0 placeholder subscription, like `pre-paid-detective-hour-pack`) with non-recurring add-on `jusprin-500-credits` at $10. `generate_credit_pack_checkout_url` copies `generate_prepaid_dhpack_checkout_url`. The `invoice_generated` webhook (status paid) creates `CreditGrant(kind=pack, source_ref=inv:<id>)`. The placeholder row that remembers the Chargebee subscription id can be a `JusPrinCreditPackPlaceholder` or a field on `JusPrinPlan`; the field is simpler.\n141\t- **Subscriptions:** plans `jusprin-hobby-monthly` (Hobby, $10, 750 credits) and `jusprin-workshop-monthly` (Workshop, $20, 1,000 credits, unlimited Standard); the `jusprin-credits-` prefix in 3.1 becomes a check on these two ids. The existing `jusprin_cb_webhook` is extended: on `subscription_activated`, `subscription_renewed` and `subscription_changed` with status active, `CreditGrant(kind=subscription, credits=<plan's>, expires_at=current_term_end, source_ref=sub:<resource_version>:credits)` via `get_or_create`, and the `JusPrinPlan` term fields are updated. On `cancelled`, `expired_at = now`; the current lot keeps its term-end expiry so a person who cancels mid-month keeps what they paid for. No proration on plan change: the old lot expires at its own term end, the new lot is granted in full. Simpler than Obico's prorated reversal and errs in the person's favour.\n142\t- **Obico discount** (decided: 20%): a Chargebee coupon with `duration_type = forever`, applied at checkout when `user.subscription.active()` is true locally (no live Chargebee retrieve; the v1 code's try/except around one is exactly the kind of swallowed error AGENTS.md forbids). When Obico's `upsert_subscription` handles a cancellation, it removes the coupon from any active `jusprin-credits-` subscription of that person with `Subscription.remove_coupons`. Annual JusPrin plans are not in the decided plan; if added later, the same code path works.\n143\t- **Auto-reload with a discount** (decided, optional for the person): a flag on `JusPrinPlan` (`auto_reload`), set from the account page after a first pack purchase so a payment source exists. A Celery task, queued by `charge_turn` when the never-expiring balance drops below 50 credits and the flag is on, creates a Chargebee invoice for add-on `jusprin-500-credits-reload` at $9 (10% off) with `Invoice.create_for_charge_items_and_charges` or the site's equivalent, charged immediately; the `invoice_generated` webhook then grants the lot like any pack. Never charge inside a chat request. A failed payment turns the flag off and tells the person on the account page.\n144\t- **Pricing page:** `web_ent/frontend/src/views/jusprin/JusPrinPricingPage.vue` is rewritten to boards 5a and 5b: \"Credits and plans\", three cards (Pay as you go, Hobby, Workshop) each with its expiry rule and its price in messages, the fair-use text (section 6), the launch offer on the pack card, the Obico price shown as the price, and the footer \"After your first month, accounts without a plan get 50 free credits a month. Your own API key stays free and never uses credits.\" If 5.4 is accepted, the same page is what the app's webview shows, with the Account view beside it. Purchases happen in the browser on Chargebee hosted pages; the desktop app never embeds checkout.\n145\t- **Portal:** `generate_jusprin_portal_session` already exists for the Chargebee customer portal (cancel, card changes).\n146\t- **Brazil:** 10 of v1's 34 payers paid with Brazilian cards and 10 of 15 failed renewals were Brazilian. Whether Chargebee on this site can take Pix, and at what BRL price, is to verify before launch, not assumed. The one-time pack is the product that fits a Pix buyer (Pix is one-time in most stores), which is another reason the pack exists.\n147\t- **v1 subscribers** (decided by Kenneth 2026-11-02 on board 1c: \"grandfathered to the $10/mo tier with the original price\", \"in addition to getting 500 credits\"): people on `jusprin-unlimited-*` move to Hobby and keep their v1 price, $6.99 a month or $59.99 a year, for as long as the subscription stays active, and get the first-month 500 on top (boards 1c, 1d). In Chargebee: a plan change to `jusprin-hobby-monthly` or an annual Hobby plan with a per-customer forever coupon that holds the old price; the webhook grants 750 credits a term like any Hobby subscription. Open (the design session's fill-ins, decision 8): whether the move happens at sign-in to v2 or at the v1 term end, and how the page says that v1 was \"unlimited\" and Hobby is not. Twenty active v1 subscriptions, 15 annual and 5 monthly. Their v1 app is unaffected.\n148\t\n149\t## 5. Account endpoint and balance display\n150\t\n151\t### 5.1 `GET /ent/jusprin/v2/account`\n152\t\n153\tNew Django view (plain request handling, on `tsdweb-prod`, next to `/models`), bearer-authenticated like `/models`:\n154\t\n155\t```json\n156\t{\n157\t  \"credits\": {\"total\": 562, \"lots\": [  // no trickle lot in the first month (2026-11-02)\n158\t      {\"kind\": \"signup\", \"credits\": 62, \"granted\": 500, \"expires_at\": \"2026-12-01T00:00:00Z\"},\n159\t      {\"kind\": \"pack\", \"credits\": 500, \"granted\": 500, \"expires_at\": null}]},\n160\t  \"plan\": {\"id\": \"jusprin-workshop-monthly\", \"name\": \"Workshop\", \"renews_at\": \"...\", \"unlimited_basic\": true,\n161\t           \"basic_used_this_term\": 212, \"basic_fair_use\": 600, \"obico_discount\": true,\n162\t           \"auto_reload\": false},\n163\t  \"prices\": {\"credit_cents\": 2, \"pack\": {\"credits\": 500, \"cents\": 1000},\n164\t             \"plans\": [{\"id\": \"jusprin-hobby-monthly\", \"name\": \"Hobby\", \"cents\": 1000, \"credits\": 750},\n165\t                       {\"id\": \"jusprin-workshop-monthly\", \"name\": \"Workshop\", \"cents\": 2000, \"credits\": 1000, \"unlimited_basic\": true}]},\n166\t  \"usage_30d\": {\"basic_turns\": 140, \"expert_turns\": 60, \"credits\": 440,\n167\t                \"days\": [{\"date\": \"2026-11-19\", \"credits\": 60}, ...],\n168\t                \"activity\": [{\"date\": \"2026-11-19\", \"chat\": \"Resizing a Print to Fit the Bambu A1 mini\", \"tier\": \"expert\", \"messages\": 12, \"credits\": 60},\n169\t                             {\"date\": \"2026-11-18\", \"kind\": \"report_removed\", \"title\": \"Print report · Vent Grille\", \"credits\": -25, \"note\": \"The photo doesn't show a printed object.\"}]},\n170\t  \"suggestion\": {\"plan\": \"jusprin-hobby-monthly\", \"saves_cents_per_month\": 380}\n171\t}\n172\t```\n173\t\n174\t`days` and `activity` feed the Account page's usage chart and table (boards 4b, 4c: a total for 30 days, a bar per day, a row per chat with tier, messages and credits, plus reward and clawback rows with their reason). The composer's hover panel needs only `credits` and `plan`.\n175\t\n176\t`/jusprin/api/me/` stays as the sign-in check; the app calls `/account` after a sign-in check, after every completed or failed turn on the `jusprin` provider (one small GET, no streaming change), when the Preferences account section opens, and when the window regains focus after the person was sent to the browser to buy. A cheaper later step is a `X-JusPrin-Credits` response header on the stream start, but `AgentTransport` does not surface response headers today, so the GET comes first.\n177\t\n178\t### 5.2 In the app\n179\t\n180\tSynced to canvas version 71 (Kenneth's review, 2026-11-02). The boards are the source of truth for wording; `pricing-touchpoints-design-2026-11.md` lists them per point.\n181\t\n182\t- **A chat keeps its model** (Kenneth, 2026-11-02, \"for now\"): the tier is chosen before the person's first message in a chat and never changes after. A turn the app started (the file-opened report) does not count: it runs on Expert and leaves the pickers live (3.3). Once a chat has a message from the person, the model and thinking pickers stay in the composer, greyed, and hovering shows a two-line tooltip: \"A chat keeps the model and thinking level it started with. Start a new chat to change them.\" / \"Each message you send in this chat costs 5 credits.\" (board 2c). `Composer.tsx` today disables the pickers only while the Agent responds; that becomes \"disabled once the chat has a message\". `chat-history-cache-plan.md` D5 (a switch within one wire format) is superseded for now.\n183\t- **Model picker** (`ModelPickers.tsx`): each option shows its price from `ModelOptionInfo.credits` with its unit, \"Expert · 5 credits a message\" and \"Standard · 1 credit a message\" (never \"1 credit\" alone), plus one clause on when to use it. On Standard the price reads \"free this month\" while free Standard messages last. BYOK options show no price. The option stays this short; Kenneth rejected a clause about pictures (2026-11-02).\n184\t- **A picture in a Standard chat** (decided 2026-11-02, board 3e): the staged attachment row (`AttachmentChip.tsx`: thumbnail, name, second line, Remove) carries the notice. In a chat whose model has `images: false`, a staged picture keeps its thumbnail, its second line reads \"Standard can't see pictures, so this one will not be sent.\", and a \"New Expert chat\" button beside Remove starts an Expert chat with the typed text and the attachment (board 3d's shape). Send stays enabled; the gateway drops the image blocks and leaves its text note (3.4). The host also leaves the picture-returning tools (`view_render` and the others that return images) out of a Standard chat's tool list, keyed on the model's `images` flag from `GET /models`.\n185\t- **Balance** (a new `account` host message with the JSON above): the composer shows only a small ring of what is left, no number, no animation (board 2a, \"just like how claude code behaves\"). Hover or tap opens the panel: \"562 credits left.\" (credits only, Kenneth 2026-11-02; no message count), then a bar per expiring lot, soonest first, labelled by kind with what is left and its date (\"First-month credits · 62 of 500 left · Expires 1 Dec\"; \"Free Standard credits · 50 of 50 left · Resets 1 Dec · Standard only\"), fill = what is left; never-expiring credits are drawn as a plain number with no bar (\"Bought credits · 500 left · Never expire\"; Kenneth asked why not a bar and has not answered, decision 13); \"Expiring credits are used first.\"; actions \"Get more credits\" and \"See usage\". The bars need each lot's original size, so `lots[]` carries `granted`; a `reward` lot never expires but is not bought, so the panel needs a label for it (no board shows one yet, decision 13); what the ring measures against is open (the board uses the sum of every lot's `granted`, a placeholder). No ring on BYOK.\n186\t- **Before sending** (boards 3b, 3d): the app knows the balance and the chat's tier, so when the next message cannot be paid, Send is disabled and one line says why: \"You have 3 credits; this Expert chat needs 5 a message.\" The one action is \"Get more credits\", which opens the pricing page. \"New Standard chat\" is added only when the person has 5 or more free Standard messages left (the `trickle` lot, which this Expert chat cannot spend; from `/account` before sending, from `basic_only_credits` in the 402 body after): \"This Expert chat needs 5 credits a message. Your free Standard credits can only pay for Standard.\" It opens a new chat on Standard with the typed text carried over and no earlier messages (Kenneth has not reviewed 3d). There is no \"Send with Standard\" and no \"Switch this chat to Standard\".\n187\t- **Exhaustion** (board 3c, reached only by a race between two sends): the 402 body drives the failed-message card with the same words, \"This message needs 5 credits; you have 3.\", the same single action, and the message returned to the composer. `basic_only_credits` in the body tells the card whether to add \"New Standard chat\". `ProviderAgent::http_error` passes the body through instead of its fixed text when the code is `credits_exhausted`.\n188\t- **\"Get more credits\" everywhere** (Kenneth, board 3c): outside the pricing page there is no \"Buy\" beside \"Subscribe\", because nobody can judge a pack against a plan from a button label. The savings alert (5.3) keeps \"Subscribe\" because it names one plan and shows the sums.\n189\t- **Preferences › Account** (boards 4a to 4c): the signed-in email and sign out; the plan line (\"Workshop · $16 a month. Renews 1 Dec. Obico Premium Subscriber: 20% off subscriptions.\") with \"Manage plan\" and \"Get more credits\"; the same balance panel as the composer, where the Workshop fair-use count is one more bar (\"Free Standard messages · 188 of 600 left · Resets 1 Dec. After 600, Standard is 1 credit a message until then.\"); Usage (30-day total, bars per day, the table from `activity`, \"See all activity\"); the alert switch. Where this page renders is section 5.4.\n190\t- **BYOK unchanged:** none of this appears for providers with an API key.\n191\t- **Design:** every colour, size and radius through the token file and `ShellTheme` or CSS variables per `agent-docs/jusprin/design-system.md`. The boards exist (canvas version 71, captures in `design-captures/credits-2026-11/`); the tooltip on 2c needs two lines where the design system's tooltip has one, so that is a token change first.\n192\t\n193\t### 5.3 The \"subscription would save you money\" alert (decided)\n194\t\n195\tComputed server-side in `/account` from the last 30 days of `CreditCharge`:\n196\t\n197\t- cost at the Pay as you go price = credits × 2¢;\n198\t- cost on Hobby = $10 + max(0, credits − 750) × 2¢;\n199\t- cost on Workshop = $20 + max(0, expert credits − 1,000) × 2¢ (basic turns free up to fair use);\n200\t- apply the 20% Obico discount when it applies;\n201\t- `suggestion` names the cheapest plan when it saves at least $3 a month against what the person is doing now (pack, or current plan).\n202\t\n203\tThe app shows it once as a dismissable line above the composer and in Preferences › Account, re-evaluated when the 30-day window changes the answer. Preferences › Account has the switch \"Alert me when a subscription would save me money\", on by default (the message is in the person's favour; the switch exists so it can be silenced). The caveat from the brainstorm stands: this recruits exactly the heavy users, which is why the plans give 1.5× and not 2× the pack rate.\n204\t\n205\t### 5.4 Where the Account and pricing pages render (recommend: the server)\n206\t\n207\t**Not decided. Do not build either way until Kenneth chooses (decision 12 in section 12).** What follows is a recommendation with its reasons, not the plan.\n208\t\n209\tKenneth asked (2026-11-02) whether the server should serve the account and pricing pages into a webview instead of the app building them from the `/account` API. His reasons: pricing will be experimented with a lot, so changing it must not need an app release, and old app versions must not keep showing stale prices. I agree, and recommend this split:\n210\t\n211\t- **Composer: API.** The ring, the hover panel, the picker prices and the pre-send check render inside the composer on every keystroke and must degrade when offline, so they stay on `/account` and `/models`. That API returns numbers and names only, which keeps it cheap to hold backward compatible.\n212\t- **Account and pricing: server pages in a webview.** Boards 4a to 4c and 5a, 5b become one web page under `web_ent/jusprin/v2/` (templates or the existing Vue app, rendered with the person's data), opened in a second `AgentWebView` from Preferences › Account and from every \"Get more credits\". The desktop implementation of those boards moves from the client repo to tsd-enterprise. Steam does exactly this for its store and account pages inside the native client.\n213\t- **Signing the webview in:** the app holds an OAuth bearer token; the page needs a session. Never put the token in the URL (it ends up in logs). New endpoint `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/webview_session` (bearer): returns a single-use URL valid for 60 seconds; the webview opens it, the view logs the session in with Django's `login()` and redirects to the page. This endpoint is the real new cost, about a day with tests (single use, expiry, wrong user).\n214\t- **Theme and tokens:** the app passes `?theme=light|dark` and the page uses the same token CSS as the Agent page (`bundle.css` variables), so it is not a second design system.\n215\t- **Checkout stays in the system browser:** the served pricing page hands off to Chargebee's hosted page in the default browser, as section 4 already says; my expectation, not verified, is that 3-D Secure redirects are what breaks inside embedded webviews. The app refreshes `/account` on window focus.\n216\t- **Bridge contract:** two messages from the page to the app, `credits_changed` and `close`, and nothing else, so a three-year-old app still works with today's page. The page never calls back into the host for data.\n217\t- **Offline:** the page shows a plain \"can't reach JusPrin\" state with a retry, not a browser error; the composer keeps the last `/account` answer.\n218\t- **Latency:** a remote page takes a visible moment to appear in Preferences › Account; acceptable there, which is one more reason the composer stays on the API.\n219\t\n220\t## 6. Fair use for unlimited basic\n221\t\n222\tNumbers: a basic message cost 0.4¢ to 1¢ normally and 5¢ when the agent polled the slicer, so Workshop loses money past 400 to 2,000 Standard messages a month after its 1,000 credits of expert are also used. Rule (recommend):\n223\t\n224\t- **Ceiling:** a monthly number of Standard messages at no credits; past it, a Standard message costs 1 credit from the balance like on Hobby. 600 is a placeholder chosen from the cost side alone: it caps the worst case at about $6 to $30 of cost on $20 of revenue. There is no engaged-usage source for how many Standard messages a heavy v2 person sends in a month; v1's heaviest payer at 130 chats a month is a floor from a product people had stopped using. Set the real ceiling from the first months of Workshop charge records.\n225\t- **Rate:** the existing 60 turns a minute, plus the 50 free app turns a day (3.3).\n226\t- **Automation:** the page says the plan is for a person using JusPrin, not for scripts or shared accounts; the ledger gives the numbers to act on, and acting is a human step through the admin, not a detector.\n227\t- **Page wording:** \"Unlimited Standard messages for one person using JusPrin by hand; after 600 in a month, Standard messages cost 1 credit each. Expert messages always cost 5 credits.\" Say the number. Every surveyed vendor hides it and gets the anger at the boundary (mental model doc, question 2); whether 600 is a boundary almost nobody reaches is the thing the charge records will show, and a stated number can be raised without anyone noticing, where a hidden one cannot be defended.\n228\t\n229\tThe polling fix (7.2) is what makes the 5¢ case go away; until it ships, the ceiling is the only protection.\n230\t\n231\t## 7. The two cost prerequisites\n232\t\n233\t### 7.1 Anthropic cache lifetime\n234\t\n235\tFact: Sonnet 5.5 cache writes cost $2.50 per million tokens for the 5-minute lifetime and $4 for one hour (fetched 2026-11-01). A session wrote 40k to 80k tokens in all, so the 1-hour lifetime adds $0.06 to $0.12 per session; one expiry after a pause over 5 minutes rewrote 42k to 72k tokens, $0.10 to $0.18 at Sonnet rates. So the 1-hour lifetime pays for itself at one real pause per session, and a person who slices, waits, reads and types pauses more than that.\n236\t\n237\tWhere (recommend): the gateway, not the app. `upstream.py`'s `prepare()` already parses the Anthropic body; it adds `\"ttl\": \"1h\"` to every `cache_control` block for the `anthropic` backend. That keeps the pricing lever on the server, ships without an app release, and BYOK users keep paying Anthropic's default on their own key. Anthropic requires 1-hour breakpoints to come before 5-minute ones in the prompt; setting all three to 1h satisfies that. Verify on the live gateway by comparing `cache_creation_input_tokens` across a turn separated by a 10-minute pause, before and after.\n238\t\n239\tThe slice-finished turn itself no longer rewrites the prefix since the append-only record landed (commit 1635438d4f); the 1-hour lifetime covers the slice taking longer than 5 minutes.\n240\t\n241\t### 7.2 Stop the agent polling the slicer\n242\t\n243\tTwo steps, the small one first:\n244\t\n245\t1. **Guard in the host** (`ToolExecutionCoordinator`): while a slice the agent started in this turn is running, a `workspace_inspect` that asks for `slicing`, or a `slice_report`, is answered with the tool error code `busy` and the message \"The slice is still running. This turn has nothing left to do: the app starts a new turn with the outcome. Finish your reply.\" After the third such refusal in one turn the host ends the turn (the same path as the person's Stop) and the slice-finished note turn carries on. `busy` is in the one error-code list (`Agent/ToolErrors.hpp`), and a waiting slice is an expected state, so this is normal control flow, not a swallowed error. Each refused call still costs one small request, so three is the cap on the waste.\n246\t2. **End the turn at `slice_start`** (the real fix, later): when `slice_start` returns `started: true`, the host closes the turn instead of returning the result to the model, and the slice-finished note turn carries both the `tool_result` for that call and the note text in one user message. The model never gets a chance to poll, the person sees the model's text before the tool plus the slice card, and the follow-up request happens once. This touches `ProviderAgent`'s turn continuation and both codecs (a deferred tool result), so it is its own piece of work with the tool-pairing rules of both wire formats tested.\n247\t\n248\tThe measured waste was two thirds of a basic session's cost on a debug build; a shipped build slices faster so the poll count is lower, but the fix removes the behaviour rather than the opportunity.\n249\t\n250\t## 8. Rewards for print reports\n251\t\n252\tDecided: credits of meaningful value for a photo plus detailed text; this audience did not game Obico's labelling rewards; keep a human review flag.\n253\t\n254\t- **Where the button lives** (boards 8a to 8d): the Print history entry gets \"Tell us how it printed · 25 credits\", the reward named in the button. People come back to Home with the part in hand, so a project card on Home shows the same line when its latest G-code has finished and has no report, and opens Print history filtered to that project; the line goes away after two weeks (a guess at the limit). The one-line G-code marker in a chat keeps its \"history\" link and gets no button. The form (8b) opens from the entry and names it (\"Plate 1 · a71f 8c04 → Bambu X1C\"), says \"A photo of the printed object and a few sentences earn 25 credits.\", and takes one or more photos, a text of at least 200 characters (the form says \"a few sentences\"), and the person's own answer to \"How did it come out?\" (It worked / Partly / It didn't work), which is the report's outcome, not a print state: Print history stays without a failure state (decided 2026-10-27). After sending, the entry's button becomes the row \"Report sent · 16:10 · 25 credits added\" (8c), since Print history has no composer to show a line in. The record's facts go along automatically: G-code hash, printer, material, process, slice statistics, the plate's manufacturing input hash and, when the person agrees, the project's setup diff from the checkpoint.\n255\t- **Endpoint:** `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/print_reports` (multipart, bearer token, plain Django on `tsdweb`). Stores a `PrintReport` row and the photos in the object store the way `UploadedTimelapse` stores videos. One report per `(user, gcode_hash)`; at most three rewarded reports a day per person.\n256\t- **Reward:** 25 credits (50¢ at pack price, half an expert session) as `CreditGrant(kind=reward, source_ref=report:<id>)`, never expiring, granted at once when the automatic checks pass (photo present and decodable, text length, not a duplicate). The app shows \"+25 credits\" in the balance.\n257\t- **Review:** `PrintReportReview(report, invalid, note)` with an admin page like `EntPrintShotFeedbackAdmin`, filterable by unreviewed. Marking one invalid writes a `-25` `adjustment` lot (balance can go to zero, not below, for the rest), shown in the Account usage table as a row with the note as its reason (\"Removed: the photo doesn't show a printed object.\", board 4c), and after two invalid reports the person's reports stop earning. The invalid share is the number to watch; Obico's `PrintShotFeedbackReview.invalid` share can be counted now for a baseline (offered earlier, not yet done).\n258\t- **Why not in the gateway stream:** reports are not chats; they are the training-data channel, so they get their own table and their own retention rule.\n259\t\n260\t## 9. Sign-in, accounts and the v1 free quota\n261\t\n262\t- The sign-up lot is created on first contact (3.5), so no change to the OAuth flow.\n263\t- v1's `JusPrinAICredit` keeps serving the v1 app. The v2 gateway stops calling `consume_credit_for_pipeline`; its `quota_exhausted` code stays in `errors.py` for v1 compatibility but is no longer raised by v2.\n264\t- Account deletion: `can_account_be_deleted` in `app_ent.models` blocks deletion while a prepaid detection-hour pack exists; credit lots should not block deletion (unused credits are forfeited, said on the page).\n265\t\n266\t## 10. Measuring it\n267\t\n268\t- `_Turn._log` already writes one Langfuse generation per request with token usage; add `credits`, `tier`, `origin` and `charge_id` to its metadata so cost per credit is one query.\n269\t- A nightly Celery task computes, per tier, upstream cost (from captured `usage` and the price table) against credits charged for the day, and the share of subscription credits forfeited. These are hypotheses 1 to 3 of the mental model measured on real people instead of on four harness runs.\n270\t- Alerts: margin per tier below 20% for three days; free app turns per person above the daily cap; reward invalid share above 10%.\n271\t\n272\t## 11. Order of work and tests\n273\t\n274\t0. **Port the onboarding branch onto `jusprin-newui`** (app only; no dependency on the ledger). The branch `onboarding-frontend-only` (one commit, `8f4588beae`, 2026-10-06, same on `origin`) adds the first-run path to Home: welcome, import presets from an OrcaSlicer install, confirm or choose a printer, open a project or the bundled example. It was built on `4389492239` and is 139 commits behind; a dry-run `git rebase jusprin-newui` on 2026-11-02 stopped with conflicts in 18 of its 27 files (`HomeBackend.hpp`, `HomeHost.cpp/.hpp`, `HomeSnapshot.hpp`, `OrcaHomeBackend.cpp/.hpp`, `home/protocol.json`, `App.tsx`, `App.test.tsx`, `bridge/protocol.ts` and its test, `store.ts`, `styles.css`, `styles.test.ts`, `tokens.ts`, `sources.cmake`, `test_home_host.cpp`, `test_home_snapshot.cpp`). The Home side changed under it: the Home v3 page (`dbec3e3a38`), Print history (`ebbb2f8894`), JusPrin's own Preferences window (`641c17f13e`), every opened file becomes a new project (`d921c4b3dd`), and the token sync (`7671e48504`). Rebase the one commit, resolve by keeping both sides' additions, then bring the screens to the current design: the canvas's \"On boarding experience\" page is the source of truth (Figma is retired), boards 1a to 1d put the first-month offer on the setup row, the welcome screen and the welcome-back window, and the branch's \"Set up JusPrin\" and \"Set up with Agent\" links to jusprin.com/account become the one setup flow in Preferences › Agent (decided 2026-10-23). Tests the branch carried (Home page Vitest, `test_home_host`, `test_home_snapshot`, `test_onboarding_model`, `test_brand_tokens`) must pass again; its two unverified paths (a partial preset import, staying on Home when the bundled example fails to open) still need to be shown in the real app.\n275\t1. **Ledger and charging** (gateway + obico-server migration): tables, `charge_turn`, draw order, lazy trickle, sign-up lot, `credits_exhausted` 402 body, `tier`/`credits`/`images` in aliases, `slicer-basic` alias with image stripping, `/account`. Tests in `web_ent/jusprin/v2/tests/`: draw order (expiring first, basic-only skipped for expert, never-expiring last), idempotent charge on a retried first request, two concurrent first requests of different turns against one lot (`select_for_update`), expiry at the boundary, free app turns and their daily cap, the 402 body, image stripping only for `images: false`, and a `/models` response carrying prices. Gateway test harness pattern: `test_gateway.py` with the MockTransport injection the file already uses.\n276\t2. **App:** `X-JusPrin-Request: note`, `credits` in `ModelOptionInfo` and the pickers with \"a message\", pickers greyed once a chat has a message from the person with the tooltip, app-started turns on the Expert alias without storing the chat's model, the attachment row's picture notice and \"New Expert chat\" button, picture tools left out of a Standard chat's tool list, the `/account` fetch, the ring and hover panel, the pre-send check with \"Get more credits\" and the conditional \"New Standard chat\", the failed card, the webview that opens the served Account and pricing page (5.4). Vitest for the page (`ModelPickers.test.tsx`, a new `BalancePanel.test.tsx`, a composer test for the disabled Send and its line), Catch2 for `ProviderAgent`'s header and 402 handling, a Catch2 test that an app-started turn in a fresh chat leaves `chat_model` unset and the next person's message sets it, a Vitest for the attachment row in a Standard chat, one live end-to-end run through the shell harness against staging (`app-stg.obico.io` runs the same image) at a stated spend.\n277\t3. **Chargebee and the served pages:** plans, add-ons, coupon, checkout views, webhook branches, the Account and pricing page with the `webview_session` login (5.4), v1-subscriber carry-over. Tests with recorded webhook payloads, including a repeated `subscription_renewed` (no double lot), a cancellation mid-term (lot keeps its expiry), an `invoice_generated` for the pack, and the Obico-cancel coupon removal. Staging run with Chargebee's test site before production.\n278\t4. **Cost prerequisites:** 1-hour ttl in `prepare()` (test: the body sent upstream carries `ttl` on every `cache_control`; live check of cache write counts across a 10-minute pause), then the polling guard (host test: third refused poll ends the turn; the slice-finished note turn still runs).\n279\t5. **Rewards:** endpoint, storage, admin, the Print history button and form, the Home card line. Tests: duplicate hash rejected, text too short rejected, daily cap, invalid review claws back once and shows its reason in `activity`.\n280\t6. **Alert and auto-reload and launch bonus:** suggestion maths with unit tests on the four cases (Pay as you go cheaper, Hobby cheaper, Workshop cheaper, Obico discount flips the answer); auto-reload task with a failed payment path; `JUSPRIN_LAUNCH_DOUBLE_UNTIL`.\n281\t\n282\t**Ready to hand off (assessed 2026-11-02):** steps 0, 1, 2 (except the Account and pricing webview), 4 and 5 are specified to the level a coding agent can build and test from this document plus the design doc and the boards. Step 3 waits on decision 12 (webview or in-app pages), decision 10 (the domain the app opens) and the open parts of decision 8 (when v1 subscribers move and how to word it); the webview part of step 2 waits with it. Decisions 1, 2, 4 to 7 and 13 to 16 have a recommended or placeholder answer written here and do not block the build. The handoff brief is `credits-and-onboarding-handoff-2026-11.md`.\n283\t\n284\tSteps 1, 3 and 4 touch `tsd-enterprise` and `ops` (the ConfigMap gains the second alias and the OpenRouter key env; both deployments restart). Step 2 touches the app. Steps 1 and 2 can be built in parallel once the `/account` shape and the 402 body are agreed, which this document is for.\n285\t\n286\t## 12. Decisions still Kenneth's\n287\t\n288\tEach with my recommendation; none blocks step 1's tables.\n289\t\n290\t1. **Default tier for a new chat:** CLOSED 2026-11-02, Standard after the first month. Expert while first-month credits last, then Standard, as the boards draw it (6a); or the last chosen tier after the first month.\n291\t2. **Alias ids:** keep `slicer-1` as Expert and add `slicer-basic` (recommend), or rename both.\n292\t3. **App-started turns:** CLOSED 2026-11-02: free, on Expert, no daily cap.\n293\t4. **Trickle size:** CLOSED 2026-11-02: 50 ordinary credits a month, expiring a month after grant. 50 basic-only credits a month as the launch placeholder (recommend), reset from charge records after a month.\n294\t5. **Fair-use ceiling on Workshop:** CLOSED 2026-11-02 evening, no ceiling. 600 Standard messages a month as the launch placeholder, stated on the page (recommend), reset from charge records.\n295\t6. **Reward size:** 25 credits per accepted report, three a day (recommend); 10 to 20 was the range in the draft.\n296\t7. **Launch discount form:** CLOSED 2026-11-02: kept, server flag. double credits on packs by server flag (recommend) or a Chargebee coupon.\n297\t8. **v1 unlimited subscribers:** decided, Hobby at their original price plus the 500 (section 4). Still open: the move at sign-in or at term end (recommend sign-in, since the v2 app is where they will be), and the wording about v1's \"unlimited\".\n298\t9. **Pix and BRL pricing:** verify Chargebee's support on this site before promising it.\n299\t10. **Domain:** still open from the gateway rollout; the pricing page URL the app opens moves with it.\n300\t11. **Obico plan name on 4c and 5b:** Kenneth asked for \"Obico Premium Subscriber\" (board 5b). One note before it is built: Obico's own UI says \"Pro plan\" 284 times against 67 \"Premium\" (\"AI Premium\" is the Chargebee plan's internal name), so an Obico customer may know the plan as \"Pro\". His call stands unless he changes it.\n301\t12. **Account and pricing page in a webview served by the server** (recommend, 5.4) or built into the app from `/account`.\n302\t13. **Bought credits with no bar** CLOSED 2026-11-02: a bar, grouped by expiry. (as drawn; the design session's recommendation) or a bar; what the composer's ring measures against; and what the panel calls reward credits, which never expire but are not bought.\n303\t14. **\"New Standard chat\"** CLOSED 2026-11-02 evening, removed with board 3d. carrying only the typed text, and whether board 3d stays at all.\n304\t15. **A picture staged in a new Standard chat** before its first message, while the picker still works: the full 3e row, or only the sentence (not drawn).\n305\t16. **Thinking picker on Standard:** CLOSED 2026-11-02: Expert only. show it only on Expert (the brainstorm session's suggestion, since Standard has one price) or on both tiers.\n306\t\n307\tDecided during the review (2026-11-02), so no longer here: a chat keeps its model; prices always say \"a message\"; the composer shows only a ring; a bar per expiring lot; \"Get more credits\" is the one action outside the pricing page; no \"New Standard chat\" under 5 spendable credits; the sign-in offer is the headline and the button names the reward; \"Your own API key\"; v1 subscribers keep their price on Hobby; the report button lives in Print history and on the Home card, not in the chat; boards live with their flows on the canvas; the cheap tier is \"Standard\" (not Basic, not Quick); a picture in a Standard chat is dropped and the attachment row says so; app-started turns run on Expert, free, without fixing the chat's model.\n308\t"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BRwveFuNzZ97Zof8JvdZFd","type":"tool_result","content":"10:25  up 5 days, 19:29, 3 users, load averages: 22.99 12.69 10.22\n Test Files  33 passed (33)\n      Tests  654 passed (654)\n   Start at  10:25:08\n   Duration  7.36s (transform 2.05s, setup 2.41s, collect 12.66s, tests 28.71s, environment 20.07s, prepare 3.15s)\n\n1859:.credit-panel-total,\n1865:.credit-panel-total {\n1874:.credit-panel-total strong,\n1926:.composer-credits {\n1939:.composer-credits-actions {\n.composer-credits {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  gap: 4px;\n  margin-bottom: 8px;\n  padding: 8px 12px;\n  border-radius: var(--radius-medium);\n  background: var(--surface-selected);\n  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--border-subtle);\n  color: var(--text-primary);\n  font: var(--font-body-small);\n}","is_error":false}]}}
